(ROOT/'stability_definition_and_implementation_audit.md').write_text('''# Stability definition and implementation audit\n\nHistorical script `scripts/stability_subsample.py` and its protocol were inspected, and saved outputs were recomputed. For each cohort, method, and mode, 30 class-stratified, without-replacement 80% subsamples were generated using split seeds 5000–5029. Actual sizes are floor(0.8N): 48, 49, 57, 202, 81, 66 for CNS, Colon, Leukemia, Ovarian, Prostate, SRBCT. Splits are generated once per cohort/repeat and reused across methods and real/null runs.\n\nFor each of 30 partitions, external alignment is ARI between its predicted labels and reference y restricted to the same retained row indices. Stability is the arithmetic mean of ARI over all C(30,2)=435 pairs of predicted label vectors, each restricted to the intersection of their retained row indices. ARI is permutation invariant; no label matching is needed. The 435 pair values are dependent: they reuse partitions and often share samples. They are not independent replicates or an inferential sample size. The per-repeat ARIs and pairwise stability calculations are paired to the same cohort/method and split schedule; there are 30 accuracy observations and 435 derived pair comparisons per cell.\n\nReference labels determine the stratified sampling, so the perturbation population is label-informed even though y is not passed to clustering. Fixed model seed 0 means repeated perturbation assesses sample/subsample sensitivity, not random initialization sensitivity. Algorithm budgets differ by method. Overlapping draws are extensive; saved outputs show one unique split pattern across methods for each cohort/repeat (shared by design), while overlap between repeat pairs varies.\n\nPotential effects: ARI can reward coarse or imbalanced partitions depending on data and K; pairwise ARI on intersections can change the evaluated population from pair to pair. Different target K values (2 except SRBCT K=4) affect difficulty. Singleton/missing-sample behavior is intersection restriction; no missing row is imputed. Fit exceptions are converted to all-zero labels and accuracy zero in the historical routine, and aggregation does not filter status, but all 4,320 recovered records report success, so this path did not affect this run. Permuted null independently permutes feature columns per repeat using seeds 6000+repeat, so cross-repeat null stability compares distinct null realizations.\n\n**Judgment:** reconstructed implementation is internally consistent with the saved records and measures reproducibility under this specific stratified subsampling protocol. It is not a general stability property, independent-replicate estimate, or algorithm-seed robustness assessment.\n''',encoding='utf8')
(ROOT/'stability_accuracy_reconstruction.md').write_text(f'''# Stability–accuracy reconstruction\n\nThe FYP recovery added the lowest-level available artifacts. Recomputed from 4,320 saved partitions and per-fit records: 6 cohorts × 12 methods × 30 real/null repeats. For each real cell, stability is the mean of 435 pairwise intersection ARIs; external ARI is the mean of 30 repeat-specific ARIs. Cell gap is **cell mean stability minus cell mean external ARI**. The reported aggregate gap averages the 72 cell gaps equally (cell-weighted); it is not a single paired-replicate subtraction or “excess validity” quantity.\n\n- Mean cell stability: {summary['mean_stability']:.10f}\n- Mean cell external ARI: {summary['mean_external_ari']:.10f}\n- Mean cell gap: {summary['mean_gap']:.10f}\n- Median cell gap: {summary['median_gap']:.10f}\n- Independently recalculated null stability: 0.0066196808\n- Per-cell underlying observations: 30 repeat ARIs and 435 dependent pairwise ARIs; all methods share cohort/repeat partitions.\n\nAll 72 saved summary cells agree with partition recomputation within 1.12e-16 for stability and 9.72e-17 for ARI; per-repeat ARIs match saved fit records exactly. All 4,320 fit statuses are success; no malformed records or split mismatches. Thus the previous reported mean gap 0.464274 and median 0.448407 are **numerically confirmed from recovered local run-level artifacts**, subject to unverified cache-to-run lineage and non-public availability. This did not rerun model fits.\n\nEqual-cell mean gap is also equal-weight across datasets and methods because the grid is balanced (6×12). Dataset-weighted and method-weighted means therefore equal the cell-weighted mean here; that coincidence would not hold with missing cells. Per-cohort and per-method means are in `cohort_and_method_results.csv`; every cell with repeat counts and source agreement is in `recomputed_stability_accuracy.csv`. Leave-one-out descriptive sensitivities are in `statistical_sensitivity_results.csv`.\n''',encoding='utf8')
(ROOT/'nine_cell_discrepancy_report.md').write_text('''# Nine-cell subset discrepancy\n\nThe stored Boolean flag marks **20** rows: Rule (i), stability ≥0.50 and mean ARI ≤0.05. It is not the nine-cell selection. Recovered `audit_degeneracy.py` applies the later Rule (i′): a cell is nondegenerate when at least 27/30 repeats avoid either observed K below target K or minimum cluster fraction below 5%. Applying this post-hoc screen to the 20 Rule (i) candidates excludes CoreHalo–Prostate (one cluster throughout) and CoHiRF–Prostate (18/30 degenerate), leaving **18**.\n\nThe nine manuscript cells are exactly the Rule (i′) cells for methods FCAG, FCM, and FastMVC, each qualifying on three cohorts (Rule iii recurrence). They are a further selected subset of 18; this subset can be reconstructed from method recurrence, but Rule (i′) was added after observing CoreHalo’s prostate collapse. No evidence establishes the criteria as preregistered.\n\nNine-cell values: mean stability 0.6837644; mean ARI −0.0028530; mean gap 0.6866174; median gap 0.6661357. Rule (i) 20-cell mean gap=0.7191491, median=0.7004957. Full 72 gap=0.4642739, median=0.4484068. The exact 18 qualifying cells and nine are marked in `subset_reconstruction.csv`.\n\n**Decision: QUALIFY, not headline evidence.** Retain only as post-hoc, criterion-conditioned descriptive analysis, alongside all 18 and the full 72. It is not independent confirmation. If the manuscript presents nine cells as preregistered or as confirmation, remove that framing.\n''',encoding='utf8')
(ROOT/'statistical_validity_report.md').write_text('''# Statistical validity\n\nThe 72-cell grid is balanced, so equal cell, dataset-first, and method-first averaging of gap coincide numerically. This does not make 72 cells independent. Cells share cohort data, method implementations and a common split schedule. Within each cell, 30 ARIs share a dataset and the 435 pairwise stability values reuse 30 partitions. Recovered summary provides valid descriptive cell estimates; it does not provide an independent 435- or 72-observation inferential sample.\n\nAll six leave-one-cohort-out means remain positive (range 0.3983–0.4973); all 12 leave-one-method-out means remain positive (0.4406–0.4822). This establishes descriptive direction robustness within this fixed grid, not population generalization. Largest cohort influence is Prostate (omission raises? observed leave-out mean falls to 0.3983); no cohort removal reverses the sign. Largest method influence is FCM (leave-out mean 0.4406), still positive. Details in CSV.\n\nThere are only six selected biomedical cohorts. No exchangeable-cohort bootstrap or cell-level significance test is justified. No confirmatory p-value, stability-predictive-utility null, or universal inference is supported. Correlations at dataset or method level are exploratory and have very small unit counts; the current audit does not elevate them to confirmatory evidence. The gap is a descriptive subtraction on [0,1]-scale stability and ARI (ARI may be negative), not a calibrated universal “excess reproducibility over validity” scale.\n\nEvidence class: reconstructed descriptive evidence; exploratory sensitivity; no confirmatory inferential evidence.\n''',encoding='utf8')
(ROOT/'benchmark_scope_consistency.md').write_text('''# Benchmark scope consistency\n\nThe 13-dataset benchmark evaluates external ARI performance and is distinct from the six-cohort resampling stability analysis. The manuscript's stability section generally names six cohorts, but abstract/conclusion juxtaposition with the broad benchmark risks readers inferring that discordance was tested across 13 datasets. State explicitly that only six biomedical cohorts enter the stability–accuracy analysis.\n\nLoop 5.5/previous source inspection reports 13×11=143 benchmark cells and 10 observations each (1,430 core fits), Friedman statistic 11.3427, df=10, p=0.33145, ICC-v2 rank 5/11. Those raw observations were not recovered in the FYP stability package and are outside this recomputation. The manuscript's 3,000-fit statement and 12-method Friedman p≈0.40 remain unverified; raw CoreHalo and CoHiRF observations are missing. Do not call the 11-method result a reproduction of the 12-method table. The stability analysis has 12 methods × 6 cohorts ×30 repeats and evaluates another estimand.\n\nNo verified evidence supports “stability across all 13 datasets” or an ICC superiority claim. Keep table/figure labels and conclusions distinct by dataset set, method set, and outcome.\n''',encoding='utf8')
(ROOT/'public_package_specification.md').write_text('''# Public package specification\n\nA limited aggregate-reconstruction package was assembled in `minimum_aggregate_package/` and archived as `minimum_aggregate_package.zip`. It contains the tracked 72-row aggregate summary, a standard-library-only recomputation script, manifest, and scoped README. It recomputes descriptive aggregate arithmetic only; it does not reproduce clustering fits, subsamples, stability from assignments, or labels.\n\nA full package would additionally need versioned source code and lockfile, acquisition/version/licensing instructions for each cohort, exact preprocessing and stable sample IDs, X/y checksums, the saved partitions and per-fit rows (or deterministic regeneration), config and seeds, environment/hardware/runtime, and all table/figure generation. Run-level files and caches recovered in FYP are ignored local files and not in target Git/release archives. License review did not establish redistribution rights for all transformed data; do not bundle datasets until permissions and attribution are verified (including Yale B where applicable). No DOI/archive release containing them was verified.\n\nCurrent package status: minimum aggregate arithmetic package exists and was tested in a clean extraction; complete evidence package remains unavailable.\n''',encoding='utf8')
(ROOT/'clean_reproduction_test.md').write_text('''# Clean reproduction test\n\nThe aggregate-only ZIP was extracted to a fresh temporary directory and its standard-library script executed without relying on developer caches. It successfully recomputed the listed 72-cell aggregate metrics (test evidence: `clean_reproduction_test.json`). This is a scoped clean reproduction of summary arithmetic only.\n\nA clean-clone reproduction of the original experiment was **not** achieved: clone lacks ignored caches, all 4,320 partition arrays, all 4,320 per-fit records, and upstream acquisition/preprocessing identity. No complete environment lockfile and input hashes tied to run records exist. Consequently dataset acquisition, hash validation, fit rerun, output comparison cannot be completed. Do not describe the full analysis as publicly reproducible.\n''',encoding='utf8')
